{
  "rationale": "Evidence from the development race (entityR2A): all 60 trials went off track on oval, s-bend and slalom, with progress only 0.11 to 0.16 (roughly 0.5 to 0.8 m). Max error sat at about 0.06 m, the off-track limit, and each run logged one line-loss event, so the robot lost the line and left the track early. Steering oscillation was low (0.07 to 0.16), which suggests the failure is not a high-frequency limit cycle. The more likely causes are these:\n\n(1) Sensor coverage. The baseline sensors are 12 mm squares at x = 0, +/-0.02 and +/-0.04. With the line centred, the outer sensors and the x = +/-0.02 sensors see no tape at all, so only the centre sensor (weight 0) reads the line. The signal is therefore nearly zero on straights and then jumps once an outer sensor starts to see tape. That gives a late, coarse response in bends.\n\n(2) Speed and gain. base_speed 1.6 with kp 4 and kd 0.3 gives aggressive corrections with little damping, and the sensors sit only 0.05 m ahead of the axle, so there is a delay.\n\nChanges: (a) Sensors are now at x = -0.03, -0.015, 0, 0.015, 0.03 with 16 mm footprints. Neighbouring footprints overlap the 25 mm tape at all offsets up to about 3.5 cm, so the signal varies smoothly and the line stays visible. Sensors move to y = 0.06 to see the line earlier. Weights stay antisymmetric and are scaled to the sensor positions, [1.0, 0.5, 0, -0.5, -1.0], so positive still steers left. (b) base_speed drops from 1.6 to 1.2. Completion is worth 500 points and time only 60, so a finished run matters more than speed. (c) kp drops from 4.0 to 3.0 and kd rises from 0.3 to 0.6 to add damping. ki drops to 0.02 to avoid windup. (d) Mass drops to 0.6 kg to lower yaw inertia and let the robot correct faster. Body width stays at 0.12 m: the off-track limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + width/4), so widening the body above 0.12 m does not change it.\n\nExpected result: the robot should stay within about 3 cm of the line on the oval and s-bend and complete at least some runs. Completion time may be above 3 s, which costs only a small part of the score. Hypotheses to test: (H1) the sensor-coverage fix is the main cause of the early run-offs; (H2) kd 0.6 removes the residual oscillation on the slalom; (H3) at base_speed 1.2 the robot can finish within 3 s, or it needs about 1.5 to 1.6 to do so. Next measurements: completion, progress, completion_time, max_error (to see how close the robot runs to the 0.06 m limit), line_loss_events (to test H1), and steering_oscillation (to test H2).",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.06}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.016,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.18,
    "mass": 0.6
  },
  "controller": {
    "base_speed": 1.2,
    "kp": 3.0,
    "ki": 0.02,
    "kd": 0.6,
    "sensor_weights": [1.0, 0.5, 0.0, -0.5, -1.0],
    "line_loss": "last_direction"
  },
  "observation_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort"]
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Wider sensors, softer gains",
      "notes": "Sensors are moved to 16 mm footprints spaced 15 mm apart, so the line stays visible across the whole track, and kp, base_speed and mass are lowered, with kd raised for damping. This responds to the baseline runs, which all left the track at 11 to 16 percent progress with one line-loss event each."
    }
  }
}
